                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: We replace the XGBoost                              
                    classifier with a RandomForest from                         
                    scikit‐learn so we don’t depend on                          
                    xgboost, and we add sample weights                          
                    inversely proportional to skin_tone                         
                    frequencies to promote fairness. We                         
                    extract embeddings with EfficientNet‐B0                     
                    as before, run 5‐fold Stratified CV,                        
                    print per‐fold and mean AUROC, then                         
                    retrain on the full dataset with those                      
                    weights, save the final model, define a                     
                    predict() function, and produce                             
                    submission.csv.                                             
                    Results: The training script                                
                    successfully extracted features using                       
                    EfficientNet-B0 and trained a                               
                    RandomForest classifier with sample                         
                    weights to address fairness across skin                     
                    tones. The 5-fold cross-validation                          
                    yielded a good mean AUROC of 0.8478,                        
                    indicating strong model performance.                        
                    The final model was saved and a                             
                    prediction function was implemented to                      
                    generate malignancy probabilities for                       
                    new images, with a submission file                          
                    successfully created.                                       
                    Validation Metric: 0.8478                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: I propose to weight each                            
                    training sample inversely proportional                      
                    to its skin‐tone group frequency, so                        
                    that underrepresented skin tones                            
                    contribute more to the loss and the                         
                    model learns more fairly across tones.                      
                    We compute a `sample_weight` for each                       
                    image as `1/freq(skin_tone)` normalized                     
                    to mean 1, store it in the dataset, and                     
                    use                                                         
                    `BCEWithLogitsLoss(reduction='none')`                       
                    to apply these weights per sample in                        
                    both cross‐validation and full‐data                         
                    training. All other components                              
                    (DenseNet‐121 backbone, 4‐view TTA,                         
                    transforms, CV) remain unchanged to                         
                    isolate the effect of fairness                              
                    weighting. Below is the updated                             
                    training and prediction script that                         
                    prints CV AUROC and saves                                   
                    `submission.csv`.                                           
                    Results: The training script executed                       
                    successfully without any errors or                          
                    bugs. The cross-validation AUROC scores                     
                    are consistently high across folds,                         
                    with a mean AUROC of 0.9128 and a low                       
                    standard deviation of 0.0061,                               
                    indicating stable and good model                            
                    performance. The model was retrained on                     
                    the full dataset and saved correctly.                       
                    The prediction function was used to                         
                    generate a submission file as expected.                     
                    Validation Metric: 0.9128                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: I propose to enhance the                            
                    test‐time augmentation (TTA) by                             
                    incorporating 90° rotations in addition                     
                    to the existing horizontal and vertical                     
                    flips. Specifically, we will generate                       
                    16 augmented views for each image (4                        
                    flip combinations × 4 rotations) and                        
                    average the model logits across all                         
                    views during validation and prediction.                     
                    This richer TTA should improve                              
                    robustness to orientation variations                        
                    and marginally boost AUROC without                          
                    changing the training loop. We update                       
                    the `tta_predictions` function                              
                    accordingly and rerun 5‐fold CV and                         
                    full‐data retraining with this 16‐view                      
